{"name":"Yellow Yarn","hex":"#fef6be","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"},{"term":"yarn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A twisted strand of fibre used for knitting or weaving.","Bundles of fibres twisted together, and which in turn are twisted in bundles to form strands, which in their turn are twisted or plaited to form rope."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To tell a story or stories, especially one that is lengthy or unlikely to be true."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yarn"}],"entries":[{"title":"Yarn","blocks":[{"text":"Yarn is a long continuous length of interlocked fibres, used in sewing, crocheting, knitting, weaving, embroidery, ropemaking, and the production of textiles. Thread is a type of yarn intended for sewing by hand or machine. Modern manufactured sewing threads may be finished with wax or other lubricants to withstand the stresses involved in sewing. Embroidery threads are yarns specifically designed for needlework. Yarn can be made of a number of natural or synthetic materials, and comes in a variety of colors and thicknesses. Although yarn may be dyed different colors, most yarns are solid colored with a uniform hue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Yarn_at_Folklife_-_Stierch.jpg/330px-Yarn_at_Folklife_-_Stierch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarn","wikidata":"Q49007"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow","wikidata":"Q943"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Ministry_of_Information_First_World_War_Official_Collection_Q28358.jpg/500px-Ministry_of_Information_First_World_War_Official_Collection_Q28358.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Ministry_of_Information_First_World_War_Official_Collection_Q28358.jpg/1280px-Ministry_of_Information_First_World_War_Official_Collection_Q28358.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Ministry_of_Information_First_World_War_Official_Collection_Q28358.jpg/500px-Ministry_of_Information_First_World_War_Official_Collection_Q28358.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Natural-fiber yarns used in the production of rope, Scotland, 1918","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMinistry_of_Information_First_World_War_Official_Collection_Q28358.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Fl%C3%BCgelspinnmaschine.jpeg/500px-Fl%C3%BCgelspinnmaschine.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Fl%C3%BCgelspinnmaschine.jpeg/1280px-Fl%C3%BCgelspinnmaschine.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Fl%C3%BCgelspinnmaschine.jpeg/500px-Fl%C3%BCgelspinnmaschine.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flyer spinning of cotton","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFl%C3%BCgelspinnmaschine.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/40-QWSTION-BANANATEX-WARPING-LAUSCHSICHT.jpg/500px-40-QWSTION-BANANATEX-WARPING-LAUSCHSICHT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/40-QWSTION-BANANATEX-WARPING-LAUSCHSICHT.jpg/1280px-40-QWSTION-BANANATEX-WARPING-LAUSCHSICHT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/40-QWSTION-BANANATEX-WARPING-LAUSCHSICHT.jpg/500px-40-QWSTION-BANANATEX-WARPING-LAUSCHSICHT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Manila hemp warp yarns being prepared for weaving in a modern textile factory","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A40-QWSTION-BANANATEX-WARPING-LAUSCHSICHT.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Restored_primary_level_spinning_machine_at_Quarry_Bank_Mill.jpg/330px-Restored_primary_level_spinning_machine_at_Quarry_Bank_Mill.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c